From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kent Overstreet <kent.overstreet@linux.dev>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 16:42:09 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] trim to what bcachefs uses

The kernel crate's driver model (device, driver, pci, platform, usb, drm,
dma, ...), ACPI and GPU modules are unused here, and call C that older
kernels lack or don't export - 6.18 has no acpi_of_match_device or
gpu_buddy - so a module carrying them can't load there. Their sources are
in vendor-patches/exclude; this drops what refers to them: their mod lines
in lib.rs, their helpers' #includes, their bindings headers and constants,
the prelude's dev_* macros, and the users in modules that
stay - cpu::from_cpu() (Device), UserSliceWriter::write_dma()
(dma::Coherent), and mm::virt with the VMA lookups and guards built on it,
those three behind cfg(kernel_ge_7_1), which nothing sets.

The same goes for what calls C newer than the kernels this stack builds
against: kernel::interrupt and SpinLockIrq (local_interrupt_disable() and
spin_lock_irq_disable() are 7.3), and Delta::to_jiffies_timeout()
(mul_u64_add_u64_div_u64() is 6.19). bcachefs has its own locks and time.

bindings_helper.h names the headers the remaining modules need once the
driver headers no longer pull them in: blk_types and blkdev (error.rs),
delay (MAX_UDELAY_MS), uio (uaccess).

Refreshed at every re-vendor: a new upstream module is excluded, and its
mod line and helper dropped, here.

---

